class CLI
class Connect < CLI::Command
    DESCRIPTION = 'Connect to device'

    Methods  = [ 'usb' ]
    Defaults = { }
    Parser   = OptionParser.new do |opts|
        # Usage
        opts.banner = "Usage: #{PROGNAME} connect [options] PORT"

        # Description
        opts.separator ''
        opts.separator "#{DESCRIPTION}."
        opts.separator ''

        # Options
        opts.separator 'Options:'
        opts.on '--off', 'Start with all devices off'
        opts.on '--reset', 'Reset each board once its console is open,' \
                           ' so boot output is captured'
        opts.on '--duration=SECONDS', Integer,
                'How long to capture for (default 600)'
        opts.on '--command=CMD', 'Shell command to send to every selected' \
                                 ' board once it is up (eg. spank syslog info)'
        opts.on '--interactive', 'Type at the board: forward this' \
                                 ' standard input to it, and stay until' \
                                 ' end of input rather than for a duration'
        
    end

    def run(argv, **opts)
        if opts[:off]
            off_ports = offable(force: opts[:force])
            tty&.info "Starting from off state: #{off_ports.join(' ')}"
            exsys.off(*off_ports)
        end
      
        connected = []
        threads = each_device(argv).map do |name, hopts={}|
          dev_tty = Platform.usb_to_tty(hopts[:usb])
          tty&.info "Connecting to #{name} on #{dev_tty}"
          connected << [ name, hopts ]
            ok, mismatch, inversion, not_defined, failed = 0, 0, 0, 0, 0
            Thread.new do
                UART.open dev_tty, @cli.baud(name) do |serial|
                    loop do
                      line = serial.readline
                      case line
                      when /TX and round mismatch/ then mismatch    += 1
                      # spank's report path (spank report ...), which is
                      # written straight out by the port and so survives a
                      # firmware whose syslog is compiled at warning -- the
                      # cheap way to read a bench out.  A distance reported
                      # here is a successful exchange, same as an ok below.
                      when /Distance = /           then ok          += 1
                      # "Resolving failed for <peer> (missing ...)" is not
                      # an exchange at all; it used to be counted as ok
                      # because it also says "SDS TWR".
                      when /Resolving failed/      then failed      += 1
                      when /SDS TWR/
                        case line
                        when /not fully defined/   then not_defined += 1
                        when /inversion/           then inversion   += 1
                        else                            ok          += 1
                        end
                      end
                      puts "<#{name}> #{line}"
                    rescue EOFError
                        retry
                    end
                end
            ensure
              success_rate = (100.0 * ok / (ok + inversion + not_defined)).round(2)
              puts "<#{name}> SUMMARY: ok=#{ok} / inversion=#{inversion} / not-defined=#{not_defined} / failed=#{failed} / mismatch=#{mismatch} tx-rate=#{success_rate}"
            end
        end

        # A board that is already running has usually said everything it
        # had to say before its console was opened: the banner and the
        # driver's init lines are long gone.  Resetting once the readers
        # are attached is the only way to see them.
        if opts[:reset]
            sleep(1)                    # let the reader threads settle
            connected.each do |name, hopts|
                tty&.info "Resetting #{name}"
                unless openocd('init', 'reset run', **hopts)
                    tty&.error "Device #{name}: Reset failed"
                end
            end
        end

        # The interesting output is often below the firmware's compiled-in
        # log level, and spank can be turned up at run time -- but only by
        # someone who can type at the shell, which until now nothing here
        # could do.  Written on a second, write-only handle: the reader
        # thread already holds the port, and sharing one IO across threads
        # for opposite directions is a race waiting for a long bench run.
        if cmd = opts[:command]
            sleep(opts[:reset] ? 2 : 0.5)   # let the shell come up
            connected.each do |name, hopts|
                dev_tty = Platform.usb_to_tty(hopts[:usb])
                tty&.info "Sending to #{name}: #{cmd}"
                begin
                    File.open(dev_tty, File::WRONLY | File::NOCTTY) do |w|
                        w.sync = true
                        w.write("\r#{cmd}\r")
                    end
                rescue SystemCallError => e
                    tty&.error "Device #{name}: could not send command" \
                               " (#{e.message})"
                end
            end
        end

        # Either we are being watched or we are being typed at.  A
        # duration is what an unattended capture needs; a console is
        # what a question needs, and it ends when the person asking
        # says so, not on a timer they would have to guess in advance.
        if opts[:interactive]
            interact(connected)
        else
            sleep(opts[:duration] || 600)
        end
    end

    # Stdin to the board, a line at a time.
    #
    # On a second, write-only handle for the same reason --command
    # uses one: the reader thread holds the port, and one IO shared
    # across two threads for opposite directions is a race waiting for
    # a long bench run.  What comes back is printed by that thread,
    # prefixed like everything else, so the answer to what was typed
    # appears where the rest of the board's output does.
    #
    # Lines, not characters: the shell on the far end wants a complete
    # line terminated by \r, and there is nowhere here to run a line
    # editor.  Over ssh -t that is no loss -- the pty at the other end
    # of the connection does the editing and the echo, so what arrives
    # is already the line that was meant.
    def interact(connected)
        unless connected.size == 1
            raise Error, 'connect: --interactive takes a single device'
        end
        name, hopts = connected.first
        dev_tty = Platform.usb_to_tty(hopts[:usb])
        tty&.info "Typing at #{name} (^D to leave)"
        File.open(dev_tty, File::WRONLY | File::NOCTTY) do |w|
            w.sync = true
            while line = $stdin.gets
                w.write("#{line.chomp}\r")
            end
        end
    end
end
end


begin
    CLI.run
rescue => e
    $stderr.puts "#{CLI::PROGNAME}: #{e.message || 'unknown'}"
    $stderr.puts e.backtrace if $DEBUG
    exit 1
end


__END__

==============================================================================
                                tribe-control
==============================================================================

Power, flash and monitor the devices plugged into an ExSYS 16-port managed
USB hub.

The hub switches VBUS on each port independently, driven over an FT232
serial line.  tribe-control drives that line.  For the nRF52-MDK boards on
the bench it additionally speaks SWD to them through openocd/CMSIS-DAP and
reads their console over USB CDC.  Everything else on the hub is only an
electrical load -- see PROTECTED PORTS.

    ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf flash zephyr.hex A1 A3
    stdbuf -oL ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf connect --off C2 B2 \
        | tee twr.log

It lives in /root on the bench Pi, alongside devlist.conf and the Gemfile.
Run it from there: it requires bundler/setup at startup and so needs the
Gemfile and the vendored bundle under ./vendor in the working directory.


THE BENCH
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  this host (raspberrypi)
        │
        │  FT232R ──▸ /dev/ttyUSB0        the hub's control line.  It is
        │             9600 8N1            internal to the hub: it is NOT
        ▾                                 one of the 16 switched sockets.
  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                   ExSYS 16-port managed USB hub                   │
  │                                                                   │
  │    bank 1          bank 2          bank 3          bank 4         │
  │  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐     │
  │  │  1  C2   │    │  5  A1   │    │  9   ·   │    │ 13  RPi  │     │
  │  │  2  B2   │    │  6  B1   │    │ 10   ·   │    │ 14  RPi  │     │
  │  │  3  A2   │    │  7   ·   │    │ 11   ·   │    │ 15  RPi  │     │
  │  │  4  A3   │    │  8   ·   │    │ 12   ·   │    │ 16  RPi  │     │
  │  └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────┘     │
  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
      nRF52-MDK boards, declared        power only, nothing talks to
      in devlist.conf.  SWD over        them.  Never power these down:
      CMSIS-DAP, console over CDC.      see PROTECTED PORTS below.

Ports are numbered 1 to 16 across four internal banks of four.  These are
the hub's own numbers, the ones the devlist and every command use; whether
the sockets are silkscreened that way is another matter, so count banks
left to right and sockets top to bottom if you are looking for one by hand.

The FT232 control adapter enumerates at the last position of the hub's
internal tree (USB path 1-1.2.4.4), which makes it look as though it
occupies port 16.  It does not: it is wired inside the hub and is not
switched.  Port 16 is an ordinary socket, and on this bench it feeds a
Raspberry Pi.  The one consequence is that a board must never be put on
port 16: --method usb would compute the path 1-1.2.4.4 for it and address
the FT232 instead.

Which of the four Pis sits on which of ports 13 to 16 is not recorded
anywhere, here or in the devlist.  If you need to reboot one in particular,
establish that physically first -- the tool cannot tell you, because
nothing on those ports communicates with the host.

The host is not one of them.  It has its own supply, so no hub port can
cut power to the machine you are typing on, and --force is safe in that
one narrow respect.

Requirements: Ruby 3.1 with the bundle vendored under ./vendor, and openocd
0.12 at /usr/bin/openocd, overridable with --openocd.


OPTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ordering matters, and it is the thing most likely to waste your afternoon.
Global options come before the command; a command's own options come after
the command but before its sub-action.  So:

    ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf --force usb off 14      global, then cmd
    ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf usb --default=false set C2:on

and NOT 'usb set --default=false ...', which parses the flag as an argument
to 'set' and fails with "invalid argument".

Global:

    -d, --device=DEV        serial line to the hub.  Auto-detected on Linux
                            by looking for an FTDI 0403:6001; normally
                            /dev/ttyUSB0.  Pass it explicitly if you have
                            more than one FTDI adapter attached.
    -D, --devlist=FILE      the device list.  Required for any power-down
                            at all, and by any command taking device
                            names.  'usb on' and 'usb status' work
                            without it.
    -m, --method=TYPE       device selection: power, usb or serial.  See
                            DEVICE SELECTION.  Rejected if the command does
                            not support it.
    -W, --warm-up=SECONDS   delay after powering a port on, before touching
                            the board.  Default 5.
    -F, --force             switch ports the devlist does not allow.  See
                            PROTECTED PORTS.
    -p, --password=STRING   supplies the hub password; it does not change
                            it.  Default 'pass'.  A wrong one makes the
                            hub answer E01 and the command abort loudly,
                            not silently.
        --openocd=PATH      openocd binary.  Default /usr/bin/openocd.
        --debug[=FILE]      raise the log level to debug.  The FILE
                            argument is accepted and ignored.
    -v, --[no-]verbose      run verbosely.
    -V, --version           print the ExSYS library version and exit.
    -h, --help              usage, including the command list.

Per-command: 'usb' takes --default=BOOL, 'connect' takes --off.  Both are
described under COMMANDS.

HUB PROTOCOL, AND WHAT IT CANNOT TELL YOU
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The hub speaks a small ASCII protocol over the FT232 line.  Recovered from
the vendor's own cusba64 binary (which ships unstripped), the complete
vocabulary is eight commands, each terminated by CR, answering 'G...' on
success and 'E01' on error:

    ?Q   query: port count, on mask, off mask, firmware   no password
    GP   get port states                                  no password
    SP   set port states                                  password
    FP   set port states, and save as the power-on state  password
    WP   save the current states as the power-on state    password
    CP   change password                                  password
    RD   restore factory defaults                         password
    RH   reset the whole hub                              password

The exsys gem implements all of these except ?Q.  The default password is
'pass', right-padded to 8 characters.

tribe-control only ever issues SP, never FP or WP: port states are set for
the here and now and never written to the hub's flash, so nothing it does
survives a hub power cycle.  Note also that RD, the only recovery from a
forgotten password, drops every port -- so changing the password with CP
is close to a one-way door on a bench with Pis on it.

There is no per-port current or voltage telemetry, and no way to bolt it
on: the protocol has no such command, cusba64 contains no such code, and
USB 2.x/3.x hubs do not report per-port draw to the host under any
circumstances.  A device's bMaxPower descriptor is what it asks for at
enumeration, not what it consumes, and the Raspberry Pis never enumerate
at all.  Measuring consumption needs an inline USB power meter or an
instrumented bench supply.

Two of these commands are worth fearing.  RH resets the hub and RD
restores factory defaults; either drops every port, which reboots all four
Raspberry Pis.  tribe-control never issues them, and the exsys gem only
does so via #reset and #restore.

DEVLIST
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A UCL file mapping a device name to a hub port, and optionally to the
board's CMSIS-DAP serial number.  It is what -D/--devlist points at, and it
is the only thing that tells tribe-control which ports it may touch.

    undeclared = protect            # the default; see PROTECTED PORTS
    reserved   = [ 13, 14, 15, 16 ] # Raspberry Pi power feeds

    C2 { serial = '1026360216055e5b0000...97969902'  port = 1 }
    B2 {                                             port = 2 }
    A2 {                                             port = 3 }
    A3 { serial = '1026360213072dde0000...97969902'  port = 4 }
    A1 { serial = '102636021605598e0000...97969902'  port = 5 }
    B1 { serial = '1026360202c4dc0f0000...97969902'  port = 6 }

Indentation is not significant; the serials above are elided in the middle
for width, the real ones are 48 hex characters and must be pasted whole.

Every key other than 'reserved' and 'undeclared' is a device.  A command
given no device names operates on all of them.  Names may be used anywhere
a port number can be, in every command.

A board with no serial= cannot be reached by 'reset' or by --method
serial, both of which address a board by its serial number: the run
aborts with "Device without serial".  Fix it by running 'serial' (see
RECIPES) and pasting the value in.  Every board on this bench has one.

Two optional keys describe a board that is not an nRF52-MDK (added
2026-09-14 for D4, a DWM1001-DEV on port 7):

    D4 { serial = '000760040233'  interface = jlink  baud = 115200  port = 7 }

    interface   the openocd interface script, without the .cfg: cmsis-dap
                (the default, the MDK's DAPLink) or jlink (the DWM1001-DEV's
                J-Link OB).  Used by flash, reset, serial and connect
                --reset.  With jlink, serial= is the J-Link serial number
                as udev reports it; leading zeros are accepted.
    baud        the console speed 'connect' opens, default 230400 (what the
                MDK overlay sets).  The stock DWM1001-DEV devicetree runs
                its console at 115200.
    power_cycle the moments at which the board must have its port powered
                off and on again: one name, or a list of them.  Known:
                  after-flash   after a successful flash.  The DWM1001-DEV
                                needs it: after openocd's reset init /
                                write / reset run its DW1000 no longer
                                reports transmissions (the board resolves
                                nothing and logs "our TX timestamps are
                                missing") until the module is power-cycled;
                                a plain SWD reset is fine.  Measured
                                2026-09-14: 0 distances in 30 s after a
                                flash, 709 after the cycle.
                A name nothing acts on is not an error, so a new moment can
                be recorded here before the command that honours it exists.
                It lives here, and not only as the flash --power-cycle
                option, so that a flash by hand on the hub cannot forget it.

'reserved' lists ports that must never be powered down.  'undeclared' says
what to do with every port the file does not mention.  Both are described
under PROTECTED PORTS.

There is no schema and no validation.  A misspelled 'reserverd' becomes a
device named reserverd and silently unprotects four Raspberry Pis, so
after editing this file run 'usb status' and read the protected column
before you trust it.


PROTECTED PORTS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Four Raspberry Pis hang off this hub for power only.  Nothing communicates
with them, so nothing notices if their VBUS disappears -- they simply
reboot, uncleanly, mid-write.  Two devlist keys keep that from happening.

'undeclared' decides the fate of every port the devlist does not mention:

    undeclared = protect    the default.  Only ports declared in the
                            devlist may be powered down.
    undeclared = switch     any of the 16 may be powered down.

'reserved' names ports that are never powered down whichever mode is in
force, so it protects a port even when that port is a declared device:

    reserved = [ 13, 14, 15, 16 ]

Together:

    tribe-control powers down a port only if the devlist allows it,
    and never one the devlist reserves.

This is enforced at every path that can cut power, not just the obvious
one: 'usb off', 'usb toggle', 'usb set' with a port set to off, the
all-ports-off that begins --method power, and 'connect --off'.  You do not
have to reason about which commands are dangerous; they all go through the
same gate.

So a bare 'usb off' means "every switchable port", not "all 16".  Naming a
protected port is an error, not a silent skip:

    $ ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf usb off 14
    tribe-control: refusing to power down port(s) 14: not switchable,
    being undeclared or reserved (use --force)

Without a devlist there is no safe set, so power-down is refused outright.

-F/--force lifts both rules and restores the old "all 16 ports" behaviour.
It is the only way to deliberately reboot a Pi, and it is a hard VBUS cut,
not a shutdown: the Pi loses power mid-write, with whatever consequences
that has for its filesystem.  Shut it down over the network first if you
care about what is on it.  It cannot strand you, though -- the host runs
off its own supply, not off the hub.

Powering *on* is never guarded: 'usb on' with no arguments still turns on
all 16 ports, which is the state the bench should normally sit in.


DEVICE SELECTION
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Flashing one of six identical boards means telling openocd which one.
There are three ways, chosen with -m/--method:

  ┌─────────┬─────────────────┬──────────────┬────────────────────────┐
  │ method  │ addresses a     │ requires     │ power while the job    │
  │         │ board by        │              │ runs                   │
  ├─────────┼─────────────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────────┤
  │ serial  │ its CMSIS-DAP   │ serial= for  │ every declared port    │
  │         │ serial number   │ every board  │ on; boards in parallel │
  ├─────────┼─────────────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────────┤
  │ usb     │ its USB path,   │ Linux (not   │ every declared port    │
  │         │ root.bank.slot  │ FreeBSD)     │ on; boards in sequence │
  ├─────────┼─────────────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────────┤
  │ power   │ being the only  │ nothing      │ exactly one board at   │
  │         │ board powered   │              │ a time, rest cut       │
  └─────────┴─────────────────┴──────────────┴────────────────────────┘

Which to use:

    power    the default, and the only one that needs no configuration --
             it identifies a board by being the only one powered.  Slowest
             by far: every board costs a power cycle plus the warm-up, so
             the whole bench is six sequential rounds.  It also leaves
             every declared board POWERED OFF when it finishes, because
             each port is switched off again after its turn.  Run
             'usb on' afterwards to bring the bench back up.
    serial   use once every board has a serial= in the devlist.  Boards
             stay powered and are done in parallel, so it is much faster
             and far less disruptive.  Aborts if any selected board lacks
             a serial.
    usb      what 'connect' uses.  Addresses by USB topology, so it needs
             no serials, but it is Linux-only.

Each command accepts only the methods that make sense for it, and uses the
first as its default:

    command    methods accepted          default
    -------    ----------------          -------
    usb        (none: acts on ports)     n/a
    serial     power                     power
    flash      power, serial             power
    reset      serial                    serial
    connect    usb                       usb

--method usb derives the board's USB location from the hub's own geometry,
four banks of four:

      port 5 ─┬─▸ bank 2      bank is (port minus 1) div 4, plus 1
      (A1)    └─▸ slot 1      slot is (port minus 1) mod 4, plus 1

      hub root path .............  1-1.2
      openocd adapter usb location 1-1.2.2.1
                                   └───┘ │ │
                                     │   │ └── slot
                                     │   └──── bank
                                     └──────── hub root

After any power-on, -W/--warm-up seconds (default 5) pass before the boards
are touched, to let them enumerate.


COMMANDS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every command takes device names or port numbers interchangeably, and
operates on all declared devices when given neither.

usb  status|on|off|toggle|set  [PORT|DEVNAME]...
    Drive hub port power directly.  'set' takes PORT:STATE pairs, where
    STATE is any of on/off/1/0/true/false/t/f.  With no arguments, 'on'
    means all 16 ports, while 'off' and 'toggle' mean every switchable
    port (see PROTECTED PORTS).
    --default=BOOL sets what happens to ports 'set' does not name; it goes
    before the word 'set'.  Without --force it is applied only to
    switchable ports, so it cannot sweep a Pi off.
    'status' is read-only: it asks the hub for its port mask and prints
    every port with its device name, its state, and whether the devlist
    lets tribe-control switch it.  Run it before anything involving
    --force.
    Note that 'usb on' does not wait out --warm-up; it returns as soon as
    the hub acknowledges.
    Selection method: none.  Exit status: 0, or 1 on error.

serial  [PORT|DEVNAME]...
    Report each board's CMSIS-DAP serial number, by running openocd and
    reading back "Info : CMSIS-DAP: Serial# =" (or the J-Link S/N line
    for interface = jlink).  Prints one line per
    board, "Serial for A1: 1026...".  Use it to populate the devlist.
    Selection method: power only -- identifying a board is precisely what
    is missing at this point, so it has to be done by elimination.  That
    means it power-cycles the bench and leaves it off; see DEVICE
    SELECTION.  Exit status: 1 if any board could not be read.

flash  FIRMWARE [PORT|DEVNAME]...
    openocd 'flash write_image erase FIRMWARE' then 'reset run', against
    target/nrf52.cfg over the board's interface= script (cmsis-dap by
    default) with SWD.  FIRMWARE is
    resolved by openocd relative to the current directory.
    Every selected board is attempted even if an earlier one failed; the
    exit status reflects the whole run.
    A board whose devlist entry lists after-flash under power_cycle (see
    DEVLIST: the DWM1001-DEV) has its port powered off and on again after
    a successful flash; --power-cycle, before FIRMWARE, does the same for
    any board.
    Selection methods: power (default), serial.  Exit status: 1 if any
    board failed.

reset  [PORT|DEVNAME]...
    openocd 'reset run'.
    Selection method: serial only, so it cannot address a board that has
    no serial= in the devlist.  Exit status: 1 if any board failed.

connect  [PORT|DEVNAME]...
    Open each board's CDC console at its baud= (default 230400) and
    stream it, prefixing
    every line with <DEVNAME>.  Lines are tallied into five buckets: ok, inversion, not-defined (all
    three matching "SDS TWR"), failed ("Resolving failed", which also
    says "SDS TWR" and was once counted as ok) and mismatch ("TX and
    round mismatch").  A spank report line ("Distance = ...",
    see --command) counts as ok too: it is the same successful exchange
    reported by a cheaper path.  On exit each board prints a SUMMARY line
    with those counts and a tx-rate percentage.
    Boards are streamed concurrently, one thread each, which is what
    makes a two-way-ranging capture meaningful; the "in sequence" in the
    method table above describes the order boards are set up in, not the
    monitoring.
    The buckets are firmware-defined strings, not tribe-control's
    invention: whatever the board prints is what gets counted, and
    tx-rate is ok over (ok + inversion + not-defined), so mismatch is
    deliberately excluded from the denominator.
    --off powers down every declared board first, for a clean start --
    every declared board, not only the ones named on the command line --
    and then powers the selected ones back up.
    --reset resets each board over SWD once its console is open.  Without
    it you see only what a board says from the moment the reader attaches,
    which on an already-running board is nothing at all until the next
    ranging exchange: the banner and the driver's init lines -- including
    any "Failed to initialize" -- were printed seconds after power-on and
    are gone.  Use it whenever you care about why a board is quiet, and
    note that --off alone does NOT substitute: the port is powered back up
    before the console is opened, so boot output still escapes.
    --duration=SECONDS bounds the capture; the default is 600.
    --command=CMD types CMD at each board's shell once it is up (two
    seconds after a --reset, half a second otherwise) and is the way to
    reach anything the firmware gates behind a run-time setting.  The
    case it exists for: redskin compiles spank's log level to warning,
    but the ranging results are logged at info, so a capture counts zero
    exchanges on a bench that is in fact working perfectly --

        --command='spank syslog info'      # the whole info stream
        --command='spank report all'       # just the distances, cheaper

    turns them on without reflashing.  Prefer the second: the report path
    is written straight out by the port instead of going through the
    syslog, so it is not silenced by the compiled-in level and costs the
    firmware less in a loop that is timing sensitive.  It is sent once, to every selected
    board, on a separate write handle; the command is not echoed by
    tribe-control, but the board's own echo and reply appear in the
    capture like any other output.  Give it once -- repeating the option
    keeps only the last.
    ALWAYS run it under 'stdbuf -oL' if you are piping the output
    anywhere.  Without that, the pipe buffers in 4 KB blocks and 'tee'
    shows nothing for minutes.
    --interactive is --command without the limit of one command decided
    in advance: standard input is forwarded to the board, a line at a
    time, and the console stays open until end of input instead of for
    a --duration.  One device only -- there is one standard input, and
    two boards sharing it could not be told apart or addressed
    separately.  What the board replies appears in the stream like
    anything else, prefixed with its name, so what was typed and what
    came back read in order.
    It is meant to be run through a terminal -- 'bench connect' opens it
    over ssh -t, which is what supplies the editing and the echo of the
    line being typed; a pipe works too, and sends what it is given.
    Selection method: usb only, so Linux only.  Exit status:
    0, or 1 on error; nothing about what the boards did.


RECIPES
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Read the serials of every board, to fill in the devlist.  This cycles the
bench and leaves it powered off:

    ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf serial
    ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf usb on

Add a board on a new port.  'serial 7' will not work: an undeclared port
has no name, and the tool refuses to power ports the devlist never
mentions.  Declare a stub first, then read the serial and fill it in:

    echo "X1 { port = 7 }" >> devlist.conf
    ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf serial X1

Flash the whole bench, then just two boards:

    ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf flash zephyr.hex
    ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf flash zephyr.hex A1 A3

Flash in parallel, leaving the bench powered.  Needs a serial for every
board named, so fix the gaps first (see DEVLIST):

    ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf --method serial flash zephyr.hex

Run a ranging capture to a log, for ten minutes:

    stdbuf -oL ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf connect --off C2 B2 \
        | tee twr.log

Find out why a board is silent -- capture its boot, including the driver
init lines, for one minute:

    stdbuf -oL ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf connect --off --reset \
        --duration=60 C2 A2 | tee boot.log

Capture ranging results from a stock redskin build.  Without the command
the log level hides them and the SUMMARY reads ok=0 / tx-rate=NaN:

    stdbuf -oL ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf connect --off --reset \
        --command='spank syslog info' --duration=60 C2 A2 | tee twr.log

Power-cycle one board without disturbing the others:

    ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf usb off C2 && sleep 2 && \
    ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf usb on  C2

Deliberately reboot a Raspberry Pi (the only case for --force).  This is a
hard power cut; shut the Pi down over the network first:

    ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf --force usb off 14 && sleep 2 && \
    ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf usb on 14

Flash from a script, failing the build if any board failed:

    #!/bin/sh
    cd /root || exit 1
    exec ./tribe-control -D devlist.conf flash zephyr.hex


TRAPS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Exit status.  Any command exits 1 if it aborts with an error -- a refused
power-down, an unknown device name, a hub that answers E01.  On top of
that, flash, reset and serial exit 1 when they ran to completion but some
board failed; usb and connect report nothing about the work itself, so a
0 from 'connect' says only that it started.  Beware that piping into
'tee' hands you tee's status, not tribe-control's -- which is exactly
what the capture recipe does, so do not use that shape in a script
without a pipefail or a temporary file.

--method power leaves every declared board powered off when it finishes.
flash and serial default to it.  Follow them with 'usb on' when you want
the bench back.

connect stops after 600 seconds unless --duration says otherwise; Ctrl-C
is the other way out of a shorter run.  Under --interactive there is no
timer at all: it ends on end of input (Ctrl-D) or on Ctrl-C.

A board that emits no "SDS TWR" lines at all makes its SUMMARY report
tx-rate=NaN, because the rate divides by the sum of three zero counters.
Before concluding the radio is dead, check that the firmware is actually
logging them: redskin ships with spank's log level at warning and logs
distances at info, so a perfectly healthy pair reports ok=0.  See
--command.

--debug takes an optional FILE which is ignored.  The flag only raises the
log level to debug; output still goes to the terminal.

FreeBSD is still only half usable, though less so than it was.  Hub
auto-detection works now -- Platform::FreeBSD.sysctl used to be defined
as an instance method and called as a module one, so it died with
NoMethodError before reading a single sysctl -- and exsys_ctrl and
nrf52mdk_mapping both run, verified on a FreeBSD 15.1 host with no hub
attached, where they correctly return nothing rather than raising.

What remains Linux-only is port_to_usb and usb_to_tty, which read
/sys/bus/usb, so --method usb and therefore connect still cannot work
there.  usb, serial, flash and reset should.

--method usb trusts the 4-by-4 geometry.  A board on port 16 would resolve
to 1-1.2.4.4, which is where the FT232 control adapter already sits.  The
bench keeps boards on ports 1 to 6, so this stays theoretical.

The hub password defaults to 'pass' (-p/--password to change it).  It
guards writes to the hub, not reads.
